  "summary": "Today's health-tech landscape reflects three major currents reshaping the industry. Regulatory changes are accelerating transparency and market access: the Trump administration is mandating pharmacy benefit managers disclose previously secret payment data, greenlit six peptide products despite FDA scientist concerns, and temporarily relaxed compounding pharmacy rules—moves that signal a deregulatory approach to drug pricing and development. AI adoption is expanding rapidly across healthcare settings, from 90 percent staff literacy at Singapore's Changi General Hospital to implementations in pediatric medicine and autonomous financial platforms, though institutional challenges persist, including a Johns Hopkins data breach linked to shadow AI use and ongoing questions about whether AI serves as partner, competitor, or crutch for biomedical research. Finally, policy debates are intensifying around Medicaid work requirements, with a federal judge blocking a narrowed \"frailty\" definition, while voters increasingly demand radical solutions to healthcare affordability as costs continue rising, creating political pressure ahead of the midterm elections.",
